{"type": "sena", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": "All's Well That Ends Well"}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Words of Encouragement"}, {"type": "position", "content": 3}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 16}, {"type": "level", "content": "B2.3"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Exchange information about unlikely events or fairly certain conditions that will be fulfilled in present, past or future  in spoken or written texts and in work and social contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_2", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_3", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_4", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_5", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_6", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_7", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}, {"type": "secondary_8", "content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "engenn11le16ob03re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Meaning of Perfect Modals "}, {"type": "describer", "content": "Perfect modals can have different meanings than their present counterparts. You use the structure modal + have + past participle to make sentences with perfect modals."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class = \"strong\"><span class=\"underline\">COULD HAVE</span></span> - to talk about unrealistic past possibilities (you use this structure with the third conditional) </br> </br>For example: </br></br><span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">I could have gone</span> away for vacation, but decided to have a staycation instead.</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class = \"strong\"><span class=\"underline\">WILL HAVE</span> </span>- to talk about something that will happen before another action in the future. It can also show that something will occur before a specific time in the future.  </br> </br>For example:</br></br><span class=\"italic\">I <span class = \"strong\">will have completed </span>the project before you return from holidays.</span></br><span class=\"italic\">  I <span class=\"strong\">will have gone</span> away for vacation this time next week.</span> "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class = \"strong\"><span class=\"underline\">MAY/MIGHT HAVE</span></span>- to talk about realistic past possibilities  </br></br>For example: </br></br> <span class=\"italic\">I <span class=\"strong\">might have seen</span> you at the mall the other day, I just can't be sure. </span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class = \"strong\"><span class=\"underline\">SHOULD HAVE</span></span> - to express regrets  </br> </br> For example: </br></br><span class=\"italic\">I <span class=\"strong\">shouldn't have eaten</span> so much pizza - I feel sick now!</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class = \"strong\"><span class=\"underline\">MUST HAVE</span></span> - to make logical deductions  </br></br>For example: </br> </br> <span class=\"italic\">That car is gorgeous - it <span class=\"strong\">must have cost</span> you a pretty penny! </span>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "You <span class = \"strong\">may have</span> worked here a long time, but tonight I witnessed you disparage and belittle colleagues in front of customers."}, {"type": "example", "content": "You <span class = \"strong\">may have</span> done a good job in the past,  but I have recently seen you mistreat customers."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Jose <span class = \"strong\">might have</span> opened the till."}, {"type": "example", "content": "The other bartender <span class = \"strong\">might have</span> done it."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "The Comparative Clause: Manner"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You can use adverbial clauses of comparison to describe manner (the way in which someone does something)."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Using comparative clauses helps to make your language use more descriptive and dynamic. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "You introduce the comparative clause with the relative adverb <span class=\"italic\">as</span>."}, {"type": "text", "content": "For example:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\">You may do <span class=\"strong\">as you wish.</span></br> It ended <span class=\"strong\">as I expected it would.</span> "}, {"type": "text", "content": "When<span class=\"italic\"> as</span> comes at the beginning of the sentence, it can sound quite formal and old-fashioned.</br>For example:</br><span class = \"italic\"><span class = \"strong\">As</span> you are aware, the project needs to be finalized by Tuesday.</br></span><span class = \"italic\"><span class = \"strong\">As</span> we all know, the company is moving ahead with the expansion.</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Take a look at some other examples from the text:"}, {"type": "example", "content": "I've been working as hard as anyone here. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Who works as hard as me?"}, {"type": "example", "content": " I am not as silly as you think! "}, {"type": "example", "content": "You are speaking as if you are the manager."}, {"type": "example", "content": "I do the work as it should be done."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "job performance"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "ability to carry out or accomplish a action, task, or function at work"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: We need to talk about your job performance."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le16ob03aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use when you are a manager."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "disposition"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a person's inherent qualities of mind and character"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Your disposition lately has been surly at best."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le16ob03aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "surly"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "bad-tempered and unfriendly"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: My grandpa be very surly when he isn't feeling well."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le16ob03aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "problematic"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "presenting a problem or difficulty"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Your demeanor towards customers and co-workers has become problematic."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le16ob03aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "beside the point"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "not important or relevant"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(expression)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: That's besides the point."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le16ob03aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to disparage"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to regard or represent as being of little worth"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I witnessed you disparage colleagues in front of customers."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le16ob03aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to belittle"}, {"type": "definition", "content": " to make someone or something seem unimportant"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content":